-\begin{abstract}
-This paper isolates the foundational layer of a projector-resolved seed calculus. A seed is not treated as a mysterious object that already contains its consequences. The operative datum is a typed ambient system, a fixed operator grammar, a finite or controlled seed family, declared relations, a packet decomposition, and a topology or completion rule. In the linear core, these data form a module over an operator algebra or, in the typed setting, a representation of a path algebra with relations. This formulation gives precise definitions of generated hull and seed rank; exact analysis and synthesis maps; packet transfer blocks; coordinate transport; quotient realization; and naturality under intertwining morphisms. From explicitly supplied split-product equivalences, the paper proves the semisimple multiplicity formula
-\[
-\srank_{\cA}(X)=\max_i\left\lceil\frac{m_i}{d_i}\right\rceil
-\]
-for finite-dimensional modules. From explicit primary-decomposition certificates it proves the finite single-operator formula
-\[
-\srank_{\bbk[T]}(X)=\max_{\lambda}m_{\lambda},
-\]
-where the displayed multiplicities are certified by block generation, polynomial extraction, and scalar quotient maps. Classical Jordan theory identifies these $m_{\lambda}$ with $\dim\ker(T-\lambda I)$ in its usual setting; that classification step is not counted as a checked conclusion. The paper also proves the analogous diagonalizable formula from a supplied finite eigenbasis with distinct eigenvalue labels, and gives unconditional all-size theorems for nonempty paths and complex cycles. Detailed models cover a quadratic coordinate algebra, the bilateral shift, full matrix modules, certified primary operators, and graph adjacency operators. The purpose is not to claim that every object is generated by one seed, nor that established algebra has been replaced. Its contribution is a common algebraic language for generation, decomposition, transfer, completion, and reconstruction. Version~\paperversion{} is theorem-complete in Lean for every named mathematical statement at the precise scope written here; classical existence and classification results used to produce certificates are identified separately rather than counted as kernel-checked conclusions.
-\end{abstract}
